{
  "requirement": "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the requirement is met.** The implementation is complete and correctly marked, and the tests cover the interesting cases. One minor test-marking gap is noted at the end.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `src/definition_glob.rs` is the one shared matcher. Every glob consumer calls it, and none of them has its own matching logic.\n  - `validate` (lines 1–14) rejects an empty glob. It also rejects any character other than ASCII alphanumerics, `.`, `_`, `-` and `*`. That is exactly the valid name alphabet in `valid_name` (`src/lib.rs`) plus `*`.\n  - `matches` (lines 16–36) is a dynamic-programming matcher over the whole name. `*` matches zero or more bytes, including dots, because there is no segment handling. Every other byte must match literally. It anchors at both ends, so it matches a complete full name.\n- All the consumers named in the requirement use it and are marked `{impl}`:\n  - Requirements evidence inputs: `InputSelector::resolve` in `src/inputs.rs` calls `validate` and `matches`. It skips terms, so only requirements and named examples match. It errors when nothing matches.\n  - The authoring `habeas` tool: `source_input` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` applies the same rule for access checks.\n  - CLI definition filters: `status_markdown_with_options` and `info_markdown` in `src/commands.rs` both validate first, then filter with `matches`. Both report \"no definitions match\" when the glob matches nothing.\n  - The authoring `references` tool: it validates, filters definitions and reference sites with `matches`, and reports a selection that matches nothing as an error.\n- The consumers filter on `kind != Term`, so a selection matches requirements and named examples only. That fits \"both requirements and named examples\".\n- I found no other glob or wildcard matching in `src`, and no `{impl}` code that adds behaviour this requirement does not specify. `status_markdown_filtered` only delegates.\n\n**Tests**\n- Unit test `wildcard_crosses_dot_segments_and_matches_the_whole_name` (`src/definition_glob.rs:42`) is marked `{test}` for the requirement and for the example `definition_glob_cases`. It covers:\n  - `*` crossing dots;\n  - whole-name anchoring (a prefix is rejected, and a missing segment is rejected);\n  - an empty glob being invalid;\n  - an unsupported character (`[`) being invalid.\n- The CLI tests in `tests/commands.rs` cover `info` and `status`:\n  - `commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors` covers an invalid glob, an unmatched glob, and `requirements.*ready`. That glob matches `requirements.ready` with zero characters and `requirements.xready`, and it excludes `requirements.pending`.\n  - The FIXME-summary test narrows `status` by `rules.case`, which is an example name, and by `rules.unlinked`.\n  - The repository-wide findings test runs `status` with and without a selection.\n- Further tests exercise the evidence-input and authoring-tool consumers but carry no `{test}` marker for this requirement:\n  - `requirements_inputs_track_matched_declarations_in_full_name_order` (`src/evidence.rs`) is marked `{test}` for `definition_glob_cases`. It uses `foo.*.bar`, matches a nested example together with a requirement, and checks the match-nothing error.\n  - `references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs` (`src/authoring/tools.rs`) uses `requirements.*`, which includes an example. It also checks that an invalid or empty selection is rejected.\n\n**Minor note:** the authoring-tool test above is not marked `{test}` for `definition_globs`. Every consumer shares the single tested matcher, so this is a small marking gap and not a coverage gap.",
